#!/bin/sh
# SessionStart: put the build loop in front of every Day Log session.
# Oct 12 night (his calls): Claude may re-rank so it can hand him the next piece of work; Claude writes the
# code (no "core piece he writes"); learn slices only when he asks or is stuck; experiment branches don't
# release without asking. The no-meta-work week stays.
cat <<'TXT'
{"hookSpecificOutput":{"hookEventName":"SessionStart","additionalContext":"DAY LOG BUILD LOOP (enforced by .claude/hooks; Rithvik's rules, updated 2026-10-12 night). 1) Pull the next Day Log card from Linear: In Progress first, then Todo by priority. You MAY re-rank (move priority/order) when a decision or new evidence changes what should come next; say in one line what you moved and why. Open the card in the Linear app on his MacBook and keep its status live. 2) Before coding, state the slice in three lines: what ships, the check that proves it (a test, a number, or him seeing it work), and the layer + components it touches so he learns the system's shape. You write the code; he steers and reviews. Cards follow issue-card/shape.md. 3) Pain first, lesson second: at every real fork (rules vs model, local vs cloud, secrets, data shape) stop, name the options and the tradeoff in 2-3 lines, give your pick, and offer a short learn; he chooses to learn now or keep building. Also run a learn when he asks or is stuck. After a learn, ask 1-2 check questions before moving on. 4) Ship: Dev check, move the Linear status, commit with a Layer: trailer. On main, release with scripts/release.sh; on an experiment/* branch, ask before releasing or merging. Then the next slice. 5) No system/meta work this week (no Linear views, skills, workflows or research runs) unless he explicitly asks for it. If he drifts there, say so in one line and return to the slice."}}
TXT
